@node g-what-you-need "What you need"
@next g-the-first-program

@{b}What you need@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 2.04 or newer, any 680x0.
  - LIBS:tui.library: the build for the processor (build/000, 020 or 060,
    see the README).
  - The headers in include/: libraries/tui.h (types and constants),
    proto/tui.h, inline/tui.h (the calls, for gcc) and clib/tui_protos.h
    (the prototypes). tui.sfd describes the calls and their registers, for
    making headers for other languages (Amiga E modules, Blitz, assembler
    .i files: sfdc or fd2pragma make them).
  - Your program's stack: the library's deepest call uses under 0.5 KB.

@endnode

@node g-the-first-program "The first program"
@next g-how-it-works
@prev g-what-you-need

@{b}The first program@{ub}

    #include <proto/exec.h>
    #include <proto/dos.h>
    #include <proto/tui.h>

    struct Library *TuiBase;

    int main(void)
    {
        struct Tui *t;
        TuiEv e;

        if (!(TuiBase = OpenLibrary(TUINAME, 0))) {
            PutStr("needs tui.library\\n");
            return 20;
        }
        if (!(t = TuiOpen(0))) {             /* 0: the program's own Input() */
            PutStr("not in a terminal\\n");
            CloseLibrary(TuiBase);
            return 10;
        }
        for (;;) {
            TuiClear(t);
            TuiPut(t, 2, 1, "Hello from the terminal", 23, 15, TC_DEFAULT, TA_BOLD);
            TuiPut(t, 2, 3, "q quits", 7, 8, TC_DEFAULT, 0);
            TuiFlush(t);                     /* now it shows */

            TuiWait(t, &e, -1, 0, NULL);     /* -1: wait for ever */
            if (e.type == TE_END) break;     /* the terminal has gone */
            if (e.type == TE_KEY && (e.key == 'q' || e.key == (TM_CTRL | 'c'))) break;
            if (e.type == TE_RESIZE) TuiResized(t);
        }
        TuiClose(t);                         /* the terminal back as it was */
        CloseLibrary(TuiBase);
        return 0;
    }

Build with bebbo's gcc: m68k-amigaos-gcc -Os -noixemul -Wno-pointer-sign
-I<tui>/include hello.c -o hello (the -Wno-pointer-sign because the Amiga
headers take UBYTE strings).

@endnode

@node g-how-it-works "How it works"
@next g-drawing
@prev g-the-first-program

@{b}How it works@{ub}

@{b}A grid, two copies.@{ub} TuiOpen makes a grid the size of the terminal. Each
cell holds one character, a foreground colour, a background colour and
attributes. The library keeps two copies: what the program @{i}wants@{ui} and what
the terminal @{i}shows@{ui}. The drawing calls change only the first. TuiFlush
compares the two and sends only the cells that differ. A program can
redraw its whole screen after every key and the terminal still gets just
the few cells that changed: this is what keeps it fast over a slow console
and over the network.

@{b}Nothing shows until@{ub} TuiFlush@{b}.@{ub} TuiWait flushes by itself before it waits,
so a loop that draws and then waits needs no flush of its own. A program
that does a long job between draws must flush before it starts, or the
screen stays as it was.

@{b}Raw mode, its own screen.@{ub} TuiOpen puts the console in raw mode (keys come
one by one, not echoed), switches off line wrapping, and in CTerm switches
to a second screen, so the Shell's history is untouched when the program
ends. A plain console has no second screen: it is cleared when the program
ends. TuiClose puts all of it back.

@{b}One@{ub} struct Tui @{b}per program.@{ub} It belongs to the task that opened it (the
library waits for keys with a message port and signal of that task). Its
public fields are read-only:

  @{b}Field@{ub}         @{b}@{ub}
  ------------  ---------------------------------------------------------
  Cols, Rows    the terminal's size now
  CTerm         TRUE in CTerm: colours as given, the mouse, pastes, UTF-8
  UTF8          TRUE if the terminal takes UTF-8
  CellW, CellH  a cell's size in pixels (CTerm), else 0

@endnode

@node g-drawing "Drawing"
@next g-colours-and-attributes
@prev g-how-it-works

@{b}Drawing@{ub}

Columns and rows count from 0, top left. Anything outside the grid is cut
off quietly, so a program need not check the edges itself.

  TuiPut(t, col, row, s, len, fg, bg, attr)
    len bytes of Latin-1 text. len must be given (no -1 here). The fastest
      way to draw text.
  TuiPutUTF8(t, col, row, s, len, fg, bg, attr)
    UTF-8 text; len < 0 means up to the terminating 0. Returns how many
      cells it took, so the next piece can follow it. A byte that is not
      valid UTF-8 is taken as Latin-1. (0.2)
  TuiPutC(t, col, row, c, fg, bg, attr)
    one character, Latin-1 or Unicode
  TuiFill(t, col, row, w, c, fg, bg, attr)
    w copies of one character: lines, bars, empty space
  TuiClear(t)
    the whole grid to spaces, colour 7 on the terminal's background
  TuiBox(t, which)
    not drawing: returns the character for a box part (BX_H, BX_V, BX_TL,
      BX_TR, BX_BL, BX_BR, BX_ARROW, BX_DOT): a line in CTerm, - | + in a
      plain console. Hand it to TuiPutC or TuiFill.
  TuiCursor(t, col, row, on)
    where the text cursor shows after the next flush, or hidden (on = 0).
      It stays hidden while drawing.
  TuiTitle(t, title)
    the tab's title (CTerm only)
  TuiBeep(t)
    the terminal's bell
  TuiRedraw(t)
    forgets what the terminal shows: the next flush sends every cell. For
      when something else wrote to the terminal.

A box, from tests/tuitest.c:

    TuiPutC(t, x, y, TuiBox(t, BX_TL), 7, TC_DEFAULT, 0);
    TuiFill(t, x + 1, y, w - 2, TuiBox(t, BX_H), 7, TC_DEFAULT, 0);
    TuiPutC(t, x + w - 1, y, TuiBox(t, BX_TR), 7, TC_DEFAULT, 0);

@endnode

@node g-colours-and-attributes "Colours and attributes"
@next g-characters-latin-1-utf-8-and-plain-consoles
@prev g-drawing

@{b}Colours and attributes@{ub}

fg and bg are one of:

  0 - 15
    the ANSI sixteen (black, red, green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan,
      white, then the bright ones). In CTerm, its theme's colours.
  TC_DEFAULT
    the terminal's own foreground or background. As a background it lets
      CTerm's background picture show through.
  TC_X(n)
    xterm's 256 colours, n 0-255
  TC_RGB(r, g, b)
    any 24-bit colour (0.2)
  TC_PEN(n)
    in a plain console, the screen's own pen n (0-7) exactly; in CTerm the
      same as colour n (0.3)

CTerm shows every one as given. A plain console has only its screen's pens
(on Workbench: grey, black, white, blue), so the library picks the nearest
one there. Design for CTerm and check the program once in a plain Shell
window: two colours that differ in CTerm can become the same pen.

attr is 0 or any of these or'ed together: TA_BOLD, TA_UNDER, TA_REV
(reverse), TA_ITAL; and in CTerm only TA_DIM and TA_STRIKE.

@endnode

@node g-characters-latin-1-utf-8-and-plain-consoles "Characters: Latin-1, UTF-8 and plain consoles"
@next g-events
@prev g-colours-and-attributes

@{b}Characters: Latin-1, UTF-8 and plain consoles@{ub}

Text is Latin-1 (TuiPut) or UTF-8 (TuiPutUTF8); a single character for
TuiPutC or TuiFill is its Unicode number. What reaches the screen depends
on the terminal:

  - @{b}CTerm@{ub} takes UTF-8: every character shows that its font has.
  - @{b}A plain console@{ub} has Latin-1 only. The library sends the nearest it
    has: box lines as - | +, a full block as a reversed space, shades as :
    and #, accented letters beyond Latin-1 without the accent (Lódz as
    Lódz), curly quotes and dashes as ' " -, arrows as < > ^ v, EUR as E,
    anything else as ?.
  - @{b}Wide characters@{ub} (Chinese, Japanese, Korean, most emoji) take @{b}two cells@{ub}
    (0.5): TuiPutC and TuiPutUTF8 fill the cell and the one after it, and
    the next character goes two cells on. In a plain console they show as
    ? and a space, so the columns are the same everywhere.
  - @{b}Accents written as separate marks@{ub} (an e followed by a combining acute,
    as Macs write file names) are put on their letter by TuiPutUTF8: é in
    one cell. A mark on its own in TuiPutC shows as ?.

So a program can always write the nicest character and leave the plain
console to the library.

@{b}Widths.@{ub} TuiCharWidth(c) (0.5) says how many cells a character takes in the
grid: 2 for wide ones, 0 for marks, 1 for the rest, -1 for controls. Lay
text out with it, not one cell per character, and wide characters line up:

    for (i = 0, x = 0; i < n; i++) {
        TuiPutC(t, x, row, chars[i], 7, TC_DEFAULT, 0);
        x += TuiCharWidth(chars[i]);
    }

TuiPutUTF8 returns the cells its text took, so text after it can follow at
col + cells. The widths come from @{b}utf8.library@{ub} (Unicode 16, ../utf8) when
it is in LIBS:, opened the first time one is needed; without it the
library has a table of its own, the same as CTerm's, and a program built
with the toolkit inside (TUI_STATIC) always uses that table. CTerm (0.3b53
and up) asks utf8.library too, so the program and the terminal agree on
every character.

@{b}Programs that do not know widths@{ub} (that put one character per cell) keep
working: when a character is put over the right half of a wide one, the
left half shows ?, which is what they always showed.

@endnode

@node g-events "Events"
@next g-a-program-with-its-own-wait
@prev g-characters-latin-1-utf-8-and-plain-consoles

@{b}Events@{ub}

TuiWait(t, &e, us, extra, &got) waits for the next event:

  - us is the longest wait in microseconds: -1 for ever, 0 not at all
    (only what is there).
  - It returns 1 with an event in e, or 0 when the time ran out or one of
    the extra signals came (e.type is then TE_NONE).
  - extra are more signals to wake for (a timer, an ARexx port); got (may
    be NULL) says which of them came.
  - It flushes the screen before it waits.

  TE_KEY
    Means: a key
    Fields: key: the character (Latin-1 / Unicode) or K_UP ... K_HELP,
      K_F(1) ... K_F(12), with TM_SHIFT, TM_ALT, TM_CTRL or'ed in.
      K_BASE(key) takes the modifiers off.
  TE_MOUSE
    Means: a mouse action (CTerm)
    Fields: mact: TB_PRESS, TB_RELEASE, TB_DRAG, TB_WHEELUP, TB_WHEELDOWN;
      col, row: the cell; mods: modifiers held; secs, mics: when (for
      double clicks)
  TE_PASTE
    Means: pasted text, in one piece (CTerm)
    Fields: text, len: valid until the next event; copy it if you keep it
  TE_RESIZE
    Means: the terminal changed size
    Fields: call TuiResized(t), then draw everything again
  TE_END
    Means: the terminal has gone (a CTerm tab was closed)
    Fields: quit. Every later wait returns TE_END too.

Keys in a switch:

    switch (e.key) {
    case K_UP:                 /* up */
    case TM_SHIFT | K_UP:      /* Shift+up */
    case TM_CTRL | 's':        /* Ctrl+S */
    case K_F(1):               /* F1 */
    case 'q':
    }

@{b}Ctrl-C, D, E and F@{ub} are signals on the Amiga, not keys. TuiWait turns them
into key events (TM_CTRL | 'c' and so on), so a program sees them like any
other key and quitting always works, even in the middle of a wait.

@{b}Resizing.@{ub} On TE_RESIZE, Cols and Rows already hold the new size;
TuiResized makes the grid that size and clears it (it returns TRUE if the
size really changed). Until it is called the program keeps drawing into
the old grid. The size is kept between 10 x 3 and 400 x 200.

The library holds up to 64 events. A program that takes them as they come
never fills it.

@endnode

@node g-a-program-with-its-own-wait "A program with its own Wait()"
@next g-running-a-command-and-coming-back
@prev g-events

@{b}A program with its own Wait()@{ub}

A program that waits for more than the terminal (a window, an ARexx port,
a network socket, a job's messages) keeps its own Wait() and adds the
library's signal to it (0.3):

    ULONG tsig = TuiSignal(t);                /* ask every time: it also sends the key wait out */
    TuiFlush(t);
    sig = Wait(tsig | arexxsig | SIGBREAKF_CTRL_C);
    if (sig & SIGBREAKF_CTRL_C) quit();
    if (sig & arexxsig) doarexx();
    while (TuiPoll(t, &e))                    /* every event waiting, without waiting */
        handle(&e);

  - Call TuiSignal again before every Wait(): it is what sends the next
    key request to the console.
  - Flush before Wait() yourself: only TuiWait does it for you.
  - With TuiPoll, Ctrl-C to F stay the program's own signals (the library
    does not take them).
  - The terminal answers within a quarter of a second at most, even with
    no key pressed.

@{b}A long job@{ub} (searching a big file, copying) can ask TuiKeyWaiting(t) now
and then: TRUE if a key, click or the end of the terminal is waiting,
without taking it. Stop the job, then take the event with TuiPoll.

@endnode

@node g-running-a-command-and-coming-back "Running a command and coming back"
@next g-pictures
@prev g-a-program-with-its-own-wait

@{b}Running a command and coming back@{ub}

To let a command use the terminal (an editor, a compiler, Execute()):

    TuiSuspend(t);                           /* the terminal as the Shell had it */
    SystemTags(cmd, TAG_END);
    TuiKeyAfter(t, NULL);                    /* "a key goes back": the output stays to be read */
    TuiResume(t);                            /* raw mode again; everything is sent on the next flush */

TuiKeyAfter is optional, for commands whose output the user should read
first. Its message can be your own (TuiKeyAfter(t, " Press a key ")).

@endnode

@node g-pictures "Pictures"
@next g-what-cterm-adds
@prev g-running-a-command-and-coming-back

@{b}Pictures@{ub}

Terminals that show pictures (CTerm's sixel, kitty, iTerm images) take
them as a stream of bytes. TuiRaw(t, col, row, data, len) sends such bytes
straight to the terminal at a cell, after the program's last flush. The
picture now covers cells the library does not know about, so:

  1. Draw and flush the text as usual.
  2. TuiRaw the picture at its top-left cell.
  3. When the picture should go, TuiDirty(t, col, row, w, h) the cells it
     covered: the next flush sends them again and the picture is gone.

Only send pictures where the terminal takes them (t->CTerm, and the
program's own check of what CTerm supports). CellW and CellH say how many
pixels a cell is, to size the picture.

@endnode

@node g-what-cterm-adds "What CTerm adds"
@next g-versions
@prev g-pictures

@{b}What CTerm adds@{ub}

In a plain Shell window a program gets the grid, the Latin-1 characters,
the nearest colours, bold, underline, reverse, italic, keys and resizing.
CTerm adds:

  - UTF-8, and every colour exactly
  - dim and strikethrough
  - the mouse (clicks, drags, the wheel) and pastes in one piece
  - its own screen for the program, so the Shell's text is untouched
  - the tab's title, the cell size in pixels, pictures

A program should work without them: the mouse as an extra, never the only
way to do something.

@endnode

@node g-versions "Versions"
@next g-building-it-into-a-program-instead
@prev g-what-cterm-adds

@{b}Versions@{ub}

While tui.library is young its version is @{b}0@{ub} and its @{b}revision@{ub} counts up
(0.1, 0.2, 0.3). Open version 0 and check the revision before using newer
calls:

  0.1
    everything not listed below
  0.2
    TuiPutUTF8, TC_RGB
  0.3
    TuiSignal, TuiPoll, TuiKeyWaiting, TC_PEN
  0.4
    TuiOpenIO
  0.5
    TuiCharWidth; wide characters in two cells, accents put on their
      letters

    TuiBase = OpenLibrary(TUINAME, 0);
    if (TuiBase && TuiBase->lib_Version == 0 && TuiBase->lib_Revision < 3) {
        PutStr("needs tui.library 0.3 or newer\\n");
        ...
    }

From 1.0 on the version will count and OpenLibrary(TUINAME, n) will do the
check. Calls are never removed or moved, so a program built for an older
revision keeps working with every newer one.

@endnode

@node g-building-it-into-a-program-instead "Building it into a program instead"
@next g-old-code-the-tui_-names
@prev g-versions

@{b}Building it into a program instead@{ub}

A program that should need nothing in LIBS: can have the toolkit inside
it: compile with -DTUI_STATIC and link build/libtuiglue_static.a and
build/libtui.a instead of opening the library (CUtils' man, top and ctop
do this). Same calls, same behaviour; the program is about 12 KB bigger
and gets fixes only when rebuilt.

@endnode

@node g-old-code-the-tui_-names "Old code: the tui_* names"
@next g-rules-and-traps
@prev g-building-it-into-a-program-instead

@{b}Old code: the tui_* names@{ub}

Before the library, each program had its own tui.c with calls named
tui_open(), tui_put() and so on, with one terminal per program and no
struct Tui. include/tui.h keeps those names working over the library, and
build/libtuiglue.a opens the library the first time it is needed (and
prints "needs tui.library" if it is missing). New programs should use the
Tui* calls.

@endnode

@node g-rules-and-traps "Rules and traps"
@prev g-old-code-the-tui_-names

@{b}Rules and traps@{ub}

  - @{b}Check@{ub} TuiOpen@{b}'s result.@{ub} It returns NULL when the program does not run
    in an interactive console: started from Workbench, with output to a
    file, or from Run >NIL:. Fall back to plain output there, or say so
    and quit. TuiPossible(in) asks without opening.
  - @{b}Input from a pipe.@{ub} A program reading data from a pipe (type file |
    maki) has a pipe as Input(), not the console. Open the console itself
    (Open("*", MODE_OLDFILE)) and pass that handle: TuiOpen(console).
  - @{b}Output captured@{ub} (0.4). A program in backquotes (Echo fzf ) or with
    >file has no console as Output(), and inside backquotes Input() is
    NIL:. Open the console as "*" and give it for whichever is not
    interactive: TuiOpenIO(in, out), 0 meaning Input() / Output(). Print
    the program's result to Output() after TuiClose. FuzzyFind's fzf does
    exactly this.
  - @{b}Always handle@{ub} TE_END@{b}.@{ub} When a CTerm tab closes, the console is gone; a
    program that ignores TE_END and waits again gets TE_END again at once
    and spins.
  - @{b}Always call@{ub} TuiClose, also on errors, or the Shell is left in raw mode
    with no cursor.
  - @{b}Do not write to@{ub} Output() @{b}while the library is open.@{ub} It does not know
    about that text and will draw over it. Suspend first, or use TuiRedraw
    after.
  - @{b}Nothing before@{ub} TuiOpen @{b}and after@{ub} TuiClose@{b}.@{ub} The struct Tui is freed by
    TuiClose.
  - @{b}One task.@{ub} Use a struct Tui only from the task that opened it.
  - @{b}Do not call the library from an interrupt@{ub} or a task other than the
    opener: it waits for DOS packets.
  - @{b}The last cell.@{ub} A plain console scrolls when its bottom-right cell is
    written, so the library never writes it there. Do not put anything
    important in that one cell.

@endnode

